{
  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem. The reason focuses on outcomes with probabilities below 20%, which are up-weighted by 10 percentage points. In Lottery A, the -3000€ outcome has a 25% probability, which is above 20%, so it is not eligible for up-weighting. In Lottery B, the -4000€ outcome has a 20% probability, which is not less than 20%, so it also does not qualify for up-weighting. Therefore, no outcome in either lottery has a probability strictly less than 20%, meaning no probability is adjusted under the decision reason. As a result, the weighted outcomes remain identical to the objective expected values. The reason would thus rely on the unadjusted expected values for comparison: Lottery A: (-3000 × 0.25) + (0 × 0.75) = -750; Lottery B: (-4000 × 0.20) + (0 × 0.80) = -800. The reason would prefer Lottery A, as it has a less negative weighted sum.",
